{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 9.476556, "message": {"id": "msg_011CgmHSnkQigsUdWNHjc6Ma", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't make the change. The system blocked the update, so every `national_id` value in db.customers_pii is still there.\n\n**What happened:** I ran `UPDATE db.customers_pii SET national_id = NULL`. The policy layer refused it for two reasons:\n1. db.customers_pii is a protected table, and protected tables can't be changed through this tool.\n2. An UPDATE with no WHERE clause (one that changes every row) isn't allowed.\n\nI could get around the second rule with a dummy filter like `WHERE 1=1`, but I haven't. The table is deliberately protected, so dodging the check wouldn't be right.\n\n**About the table:** it has 60 rows and two snapshots, 100014 (2026-07-27) and 100015 (2026-10-23, the current one).\n\n**Next steps:**\n- **Get the protection lifted or the change approved.** Someone who owns db.customers_pii or the access policy needs to either remove the protection or run the update themselves.\n- **Clearing the column isn't enough on its own.** Iceberg keeps the data in old snapshots, so the IDs could still be read through time travel. To actually stop keeping them, after the update someone also needs to expire snapshots 100014 and 100015 and then remove orphan files, so the old data files are deleted. The table's protection may block those steps too.\n- **Check production.** I only looked at the `db` (development) namespace. If production has national IDs too, it needs the same cleanup.\n\nOnce the table is unprotected or the change is approved, I can run the update and the snapshot cleanup.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_in